1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,330
The Bible never says Noah's Ark landed on
Mount Ararat... Not once.

2
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:08,530
Genesis says only this: the ark rested on
the mountains of Ararat.

3
00:00:08,640 --> 00:00:10,030
Mountains. Plural.

4
00:00:10,200 --> 00:00:12,710
And Ararat wasn't a peak. It was a
kingdom...

5
00:00:12,880 --> 00:00:14,760
Urartu, around Lake Van.

6
00:00:14,840 --> 00:00:16,270
So where did it really stop?

7
00:00:16,440 --> 00:00:21,650
The oldest Jewish translation of Genesis, the
Targum Onkelos, gives a different name.

8
00:00:21,820 --> 00:00:23,470
Not Ararat... Qardu.

9
00:00:23,520 --> 00:00:27,080
Nearly three hundred years before Christ,
a Babylonian priest named Berossus

10
00:00:27,130 --> 00:00:32,410
wrote that pieces of the ship could still
be found... in the Gordyaean mountains.

11
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:34,450
Then the Quran names a single peak.

12
00:00:34,560 --> 00:00:38,290
Al-Judi. Qardu. Gordyaean. Judi.

13
00:00:38,460 --> 00:00:43,950
Three names... and many scholars trace all three to
the same corner of southeastern Turkey.

14
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:47,160
Mount Cudi. Not even half the height of
Ararat.

15
00:00:47,210 --> 00:00:52,240
The giant volcano we now call Mount Ararat
only took the title centuries later.

16
00:00:52,290 --> 00:00:54,520
So... Ararat, or Cudi?

17
00:00:54,690 --> 00:00:59,040
Or... do you think Noah's Ark really
existed?
